WN.lb(26,{"mt_I4TDARhoVX":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Does asking a friend directly if they are thinking about suicide make it more likely?","choices":["No: research shows asking does not raise the risk","Yes: it puts the idea in their head","Only if you use the word suicide"],"answer":0,"hint":"What did the 2014 research review find?","explain":"A review of studies found asking does not increase suicidal thoughts. It can bring relief and open the way to help."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Your friend has said “there is no point” on {n} days this week. What should you do?","choices":["Take it seriously: listen, and tell a trusted adult","Laugh it off: they are probably joking","Change the subject to cheer them up"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is hopeless talk that keeps coming back something to ignore?","explain":"Hopeless talk on {n} days is a warning sign. Never laugh it off. Listen, and get a trusted adult involved.","params":{"n":[2,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Which reply listens rather than fixes?","choices":["Take your time. I'm listening.","Here is what you should do.","Same! Let me tell you about my week."],"answer":0,"hint":"Which one gives them room to say more?","explain":"Take your time gives them space. Advice jumps to fixing, and your own story takes the space away from them."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"What is the best order for a supportive talk?","choices":["Notice, listen, encourage help, check in later","Encourage help, notice, check in, listen","Listen, check in, notice, encourage help"],"answer":0,"hint":"What has to happen before they will talk at all?","explain":"First notice and ask, then listen and say it back, then gently suggest help, and later check in to show you still care."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Your friend messages you every night until {n} am and you are worn out. What is fair?","choices":["Say you care, agree a better time, and tell an adult","Keep replying: a good friend never sleeps","Block them without saying anything"],"answer":0,"hint":"Can you help anyone if you are exhausted?","explain":"Being up until {n} am wears you out. A kind limit is fair, and a trusted adult can share the load. Blocking with no word can hurt.","params":{"n":[1,4]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Who is a good person to tell when you are worried about a friend's safety?","choices":["A teacher, school counsellor or parent you trust","Nobody: telling would be a betrayal","Your followers on social media"],"answer":0,"hint":"Who can actually help, and keep it private?","explain":"A trusted adult can get real help. Telling them is caring, not betrayal. Posting online shares their private life."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"A friend tells you someone at home is hurting them. What should you do?","choices":["Tell a trusted adult as soon as you can","Keep it secret as they asked","Confront the person yourself"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is your friend safe at home? Who can keep them safe?","explain":"Being hurt by someone is a safety worry, so a trusted adult must know. Confronting the person could put you both at risk."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Your friend is stressed about exams and wants to talk. Is this one to tell an adult about?","choices":["Not usually: listen, and suggest they talk to a teacher","Yes: tell their parents today without asking","Yes: tell the head teacher"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is anyone at risk of harm?","explain":"Exam stress is common and not a safety worry, so you listen and encourage them to get support themselves."},{"type":"mc","id":"m9","prompt":"In the UK, which free number can a young person call to talk to someone at any time?","choices":["Childline on 0800 1111","999 for any worry at all","A friend's mobile number"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which service is for young people and free to call?","explain":"Childline is free and confidential for young people. 999 is for when someone is in danger right now."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Your friend has skipped the club they love for {n} weeks and hardly talks at lunch. What is the best first step?","choices":["Ask them in private how they are, really","Wait and see if it passes","Tell the whole group to cheer them up"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is this one bad day, or a change that has lasted? And who should ask?","explain":"{n} weeks of change is a sign worth acting on. Asking one to one, in private, gives them the best chance to open up.","params":{"n":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"Your friend says they feel low. Which reply helps them keep talking?","choices":["That sounds really hard. What has been going on?","Cheer up, it could be worse.","You just need to get more sleep."],"answer":0,"hint":"Which reply listens, and which ones try to fix or dismiss?","explain":"Saying it sounds hard and asking an open question shows you heard them. Cheer up dismisses the feeling, and a quick fix skips the listening."},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"A friend tells you they have been hurting themselves for {n} months and makes you promise not to tell. What should you do?","choices":["Tell a trusted adult, and tell your friend why","Keep the promise so they still trust you","Wait until it has gone on longer"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which matters more here: the secret, or their safety?","explain":"Self-harm is a safety worry, so a trusted adult must know, whether it has been {n} months or one day. Tell your friend you care too much to keep it secret.","params":{"n":[1,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Which of these is a sign a friend may be struggling?","choices":["They have stopped eating lunch for weeks","They were grumpy one evening after a loss","They got a new haircut"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look for a change that lasts, not a one-off.","explain":"A change in eating that lasts for weeks is a sign. One grumpy evening is normal, and a haircut is just a change of style."},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"Your friend tells you they have a crush on someone and asks you to keep it quiet. What should you do?","choices":["Keep it private: it is theirs to share","Tell a teacher straight away","Tell the person they like"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is anyone at risk of harm here, or is it just private?","explain":"A crush is private and nobody is at risk, so you keep it. Only safety worries need to go to an adult."}],"explain":{"kid":["Everyone has bad days. A friend may be struggling when there are changes that last for two weeks or more and are not like them. They might drop things they used to love, stop eating, sleep badly, snap at people or keep saying there is no point.","If you notice, find a quiet moment and ask how they are, really. Then listen. You do not need the right words or a fix. Say things like “that sounds hard” and ask open questions such as “what has been going on?” Saying “cheer up” or talking about your own week can shut them down.","Most things a friend tells you are theirs to keep. But some worries are too big to keep. If they are hurting themselves, say they want to die, or someone is hurting them, tell a trusted adult. Do not promise to keep that secret. Tell them you care too much to keep it.","It is fine to ask straight out if they are thinking about suicide. Research shows asking does not put the idea in their head. If someone is in danger right now, call 999. You are a friend, not a counsellor, so share the load with an adult and look after yourself too."],"adult":["This topic teaches teenagers to notice when a friend may be struggling, respond in a way that keeps them talking, and pass on safety worries. The signs they learn are lasting, out-of-character changes: withdrawal from activities, changes in sleep or appetite, irritability and hopeless talk. NHS guidance says a low mood that lasts two weeks or more could be a sign of depression.","The lab practises five skills. Learners tap real signs in a friend's weeks and see that one bad evening is not a sign. They open a conversation door with listening replies, put a supportive talk in order, sort worries into private or must-tell, and pick ways to protect their own energy.","A common mistake is promising to keep a secret about self-harm or suicidal thoughts. Another is believing that asking directly about suicide plants the idea; a 2014 review of the research found it does not raise risk. Teens also slip into fixing, saying cheer up, or carrying the worry alone until they burn out.","At home, tell your teen that coming to you about a friend is never getting them into trouble. In the UK, young people can call Childline free on 0800 1111, and anyone can call Samaritans on 116 123. If someone is in immediate danger, call 999."]},"familyTip":"Tell your teen: if you are ever worried about a friend, you can tell me, and you will not be in trouble for it. Agree which other adults they could go to as well.","keyIdea":"A friend may be struggling when changes last for weeks and are not like them, such as dropping things they love, sleeping or eating differently, snapping, or saying there is no point. Listen more than you fix, ask open questions and encourage them to get help. Safety worries, like self-harm or wanting to die, must go to a trusted adult even if you promised to keep the secret, and you need support too.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Private or must tell? Drag each thing a friend tells you, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Keep it private","Tell a trusted adult"],"items":[["They are nervous about a party",0],["They do not like their new haircut",0],["They had a row with their brother",0],["They cut themselves when upset",1],["They have a plan to end their life",1],["An adult online asked them to meet in secret",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagpal","caption":"Flagship lab: spot lasting changes, open the talk by listening, order a supportive chat, decide what must go to an adult, and guard your own energy.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_a6AYrbb7x4":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Ella decides the new boy will be unkind before she has even met him, because of where he is from. Which word is this?","choices":["Prejudice","Stereotype","Equity"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is she judging one person, or stating an idea about a group?","explain":"Ella is judging one person before she knows him, because of his group. That is prejudice."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Which one is an action, not just a thought?","choices":["Discrimination","Stereotype","Prejudice"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think, feel, do. Which word is the do?","explain":"Discrimination is treating someone unfairly. A stereotype is an idea and prejudice is a judgement; both can stay in someone's head."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Every child in the class gets {n} stickers, whatever they did. Which word is this?","choices":["Equality","Equity","Bias"],"answer":0,"hint":"Did everyone get the same, or did people get different amounts by need?","explain":"Everyone got exactly the same, so this is equality.","params":{"n":[1,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Sam uses a wheelchair, so the school builds a ramp. Which word is this?","choices":["Equity","Equality","Prejudice"],"answer":0,"hint":"Did Sam get the same as everyone, or what he needs?","explain":"The ramp gives Sam what he needs to get in, like everyone else. Giving by need is equity."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Over {n} lessons, a teacher always picks boys to answer and does not notice. Which word fits best?","choices":["Bias","Compassion","Equity"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is there a pattern of leaning one way, even if no one meant it?","explain":"A leaning towards one group that shows as a pattern, often without noticing, is bias.","params":{"n":[5,12]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Which is the best meaning of compassion?","choices":["Noticing someone is hurting and wanting to help","Giving everyone the same","Feeling sorry and doing nothing"],"answer":0,"hint":"It has two parts: noticing, and wanting to help.","explain":"Compassion means you notice someone is suffering and want to help them. Feeling sorry alone is not the whole thing."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"In a game, the same rules apply to every team, and the ref has no favourites. Which word is this?","choices":["Fairness","Discrimination","Stereotype"],"answer":0,"hint":"Are people treated in a right and even way?","explain":"Same rules for all and no favourites is fairness."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"\"Boys don't cry.\" Nobody has done anything yet. Is this discrimination?","choices":["No, it is a stereotype","Yes, it is discrimination","No, it is equity"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is there an action yet, or only an idea about a group?","explain":"It is an idea about a whole group, so it is a stereotype. It would become discrimination if someone was treated unfairly because of it."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"\"All teenagers are lazy.\" Which word is this?","choices":["Stereotype","Discrimination","Equity"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is it an idea about a whole group, or something that was done?","explain":"It is a fixed idea about a whole group, so it is a stereotype. Nobody has been treated differently yet, so it is not discrimination."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"A club turns away {n} children only because of their religion. Which word is this?","choices":["Discrimination","Prejudice","Equality"],"answer":0,"hint":"Did someone just think something, or did they do something?","explain":"The children were treated unfairly because of their group. That is an action, so it is discrimination, not only prejudice.","params":{"n":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"{n} children get one cushion each, but Ana is much shorter and still can't see the stage. What would equity look like?","choices":["Give Ana what she needs to see","Give everyone the same again","Ask Ana to stand at the back"],"answer":0,"hint":"Equity is about what each person needs for a fair chance.","explain":"Equal cushions did not give Ana a fair chance to see. Equity gives her what she needs, such as an extra cushion or a front seat.","params":{"n":[3,8]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"A judge gives one team a lower score once. Can you be sure the judge is biased?","choices":["No, you need to see a pattern","Yes, one time proves it","Yes, judges are always biased"],"answer":0,"hint":"One result could have many reasons. What would show a leaning?","explain":"Bias shows up as a pattern over many decisions. One low score might have a fair reason."},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"Leo sees a new girl sitting alone. Which shows compassion?","choices":["He asks her to join his game","He feels sorry for her and walks on","He laughs with his friends"],"answer":0,"hint":"Compassion is noticing, caring and wanting to help.","explain":"Asking her to join shows Leo noticed she was alone, cared, and acted to help. Feeling sorry and walking on is only the start."}],"explain":{"kid":["Some words help us talk about how people are treated. They are close in meaning, so it helps to know what makes each one different.","Start with think, feel, do. A stereotype is an idea about a whole group, like \"girls can't play football.\" Prejudice is when you judge one person by that idea before you know them. Discrimination is when you do something unfair to them, like keep them off the team.","Equality means everyone gets the same thing. Equity means each person gets what they need to have a fair chance. If one child needs glasses to read the board, giving her glasses is equity. Fairness means the rules are the same for all, with no favourites.","Bias is a leaning for or against a group. People often have it and don't know it. You spot it as a pattern, not from one time. Compassion is when you see that someone is sad or hurt, care about it, and want to help."],"adult":["This topic is for ages 7 to 11. It gives children words for what they see in the playground, in stories and in the news. The words are close in meaning, so the aim is to know what sets each apart. A stereotype is a belief about a group. Prejudice is judging a person before you know them. Discrimination is unfair treatment: it is an action. Bias is a lean in judgement that people may not notice. Equality is the same for all; equity is what each person needs for a fair chance.","In the lab, children sort sayings into think (stereotype), judge (prejudice) and do (discrimination). They share three boxes behind a fence so three children of different heights can all see a match. They flip a judge's scores until a pattern of bias shows. They pick a kind way to help someone who is left out or hurt. Last, they match eight short scenes to the eight words.","Common mistakes: using discrimination for any unkind thought (it needs an action), and calling every stereotype prejudice. Some children think equity is unfair because it is not equal. Others decide a person is biased from one choice, not a pattern. Another slip is thinking compassion is just feeling sorry; it also means wanting to help.","Use the words when they come up in a book or a match. Ask: \"Was that a stereotype, or did someone get treated differently?\""]},"familyTip":"When you watch a programme or read a story together, pause on a moment where someone is judged or left out and ask your child which word fits best: stereotype, prejudice, discrimination or bias. Ask what a fair or kind response would be.","keyIdea":"Eight words help you talk about how people are treated. A stereotype is a fixed idea about a whole group. Prejudice is judging one person before you know them, because of their group. Discrimination is treating someone unfairly because of their group: it is an action. Bias is a leaning for or against someone, often without noticing. Equality means everyone gets the same; equity means everyone gets what they need for a fair chance. Fairness is treating people in a right and even way, with no favourites. Compassion is noticing someone is hurting and wanting to help.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Think, feel or do? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Think: stereotype","Feel: prejudice","Do: discrimination"],"items":[["\"all gamers are lazy\"",0],["\"girls can't do maths\"",0],["judging the new boy before meeting him",1],["deciding you won't like someone because of their accent",1],["not letting someone join because of their skin colour",2],["paying a woman less for the same job",2]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagaware","caption":"Flagship lab: sort think, feel and do; share boxes behind a fence; flip a judge's scores to spot bias; choose a compassionate action; then name eight scenes.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_9NQEiYLQA3":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Ava's painting gets rain on it and the colours run. How might Ava feel?","choices":["Upset, because her work is spoiled","Pleased, because the colours mix","Bored, because it is just paint"],"answer":0,"hint":"How would you feel if your painting got spoiled?","explain":"Ava probably feels upset. She worked on it, and now it is spoiled. Put yourself in her shoes to guess."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"What does 'putting yourself in someone's shoes' mean?","choices":["Imagining how things feel to them","Swapping shoes with a friend","Doing what they tell you"],"answer":0,"hint":"It is a saying, not about real shoes.","explain":"It means imagining how a situation looks and feels from their side, not yours."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Zane hides a toy car in a box while Ella is out. Ella left it on the shelf. Where will Ella look first?","choices":["On the shelf","In the box","Everywhere at once"],"answer":0,"hint":"What did Ella see last? Did she watch Zane hide it?","explain":"Ella looks on the shelf. She did not see Zane move it, so she thinks it is still where she left it."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Max says 'Oops!' after his ball hits you. What is the best first thought?","choices":["Maybe it was an accident","He did it on purpose","I should kick it at him"],"answer":0,"hint":"What does 'Oops!' tell you?","explain":"'Oops!' is a clue that it was an accident. Checking his side first stops a fight before it starts."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"{n} children want the same book. Which question helps you see the others' side?","choices":["Why do you want it today?","Why are you so greedy?","Can't you just go away?"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which question asks about their reasons?","explain":"Asking why they want it today lets the {n} children share their reasons, and then you can find a fair plan.","params":{"n":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Grandpa jumps when the fireworks go bang. You love fireworks. What is true?","choices":["You can each feel a different way","Grandpa is wrong to jump","You must stop liking fireworks"],"answer":0,"hint":"Can two people feel two ways about the same thing?","explain":"Two people can feel different ways about the same thing. Loud bangs can startle some people, even if you enjoy them."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Mo's best friend moved away {n} days ago. Mo is quiet at play time. What could you say?","choices":["You must miss your friend. Want to play?","Cheer up, it's not a big deal.","Why are you so boring?"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which reply names how Mo might feel?","explain":"Naming the feeling ('you must miss your friend') shows you see Mo's side, then the offer to play helps.","params":{"n":[2,6]}}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Lily's balloon floats away into the sky. How might Lily feel?","choices":["Sad, because she lost it","Happy, because it flew high","Nothing, it is only a balloon"],"answer":0,"hint":"Picture it was your balloon. How would you feel?","explain":"Lily probably feels sad, because she has lost something she liked. Asking how you would feel helps you guess."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"Omar puts {n} sweets in his bag and goes outside. His sister moves them to a jar. Where will Omar look first?","choices":["In his bag","In the jar","He will just know"],"answer":0,"hint":"Did Omar see his sister move the sweets?","explain":"Omar looks in his bag. He did not see the {n} sweets being moved, so he still thinks they are where he left them.","params":{"n":[3,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Ruby loves swimming. Finn had a scare in a pool last year. The class goes swimming. Why might they feel differently?","choices":["What happened to them before is different","One of them is wrong","Feelings are always the same for everyone"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about what each child remembers.","explain":"Finn's scare last year makes swimming feel worrying, while Ruby has good memories. Past events shape how people feel now."},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Your friend waited {n} minutes for the computer and you are on it. What shows you see their side?","choices":["You waited a long time. I will finish soon.","I was here first, so tough.","Stop going on about it."],"answer":0,"hint":"Which reply says what your friend feels or did?","explain":"Saying 'you waited a long time' shows you have thought about their {n}-minute wait. Then you can offer a fair fix.","params":{"n":[5,15]}}],"explain":{"kid":["Ben built a big tower. Zara bumped it and it fell. Ben feels sad. Zara feels sorry. It is one moment, but they feel two ways. To see how someone feels, put yourself in their shoes. Ask: how would I feel if that were me?","People only know what they saw. Mia did not see Tom move her cookie. So she looks where she left it, not where it is now.","What has happened before counts too. Sam loves dogs. Kit was knocked over by one, so a big dog scares her.","When you see their side, you can say kind words. Say what they feel. Then find a fair fix."],"adult":["Perspective-taking means working out what another person feels, thinks and wants from where they stand, not from where you stand. The IB PYP strand for personal, social and physical education asks children of this age to see that others have their own feelings and views. They should use this when they play, read stories and sort out a quarrel.","It has a thinking part and a feeling part. The thinking part is often called theory of mind. It means knowing that people act on what they believe, even when that belief is out of date. The classic test asks where someone will look for a thing that was moved while they were away. The feeling part is empathy: picturing how the moment feels to them. That depends on their past, their likes and their fears.","Common mistakes: thinking everyone feels what you would feel, so a child who loves dogs expects every child to be thrilled by one. Forgetting that the other person did not see what you saw. Calling a feeling wrong instead of asking where it comes from. Jumping to 'on purpose' when the clues point to an accident. A good habit is to name the other person's feeling out loud before you say your own.","In the lab your child gives each child in a story the feeling that fits. They guess where someone will look after missing a change, and sort both sides of a quarrel over a swing. They match three reactions to one dog, then pick the reply that shows they have seen the other side."]},"familyTip":"When you read a story together, pause at a big moment and ask two questions: 'How does this character feel?' and 'Does the other character know what we know?' Then swap: ask your child to guess how you felt about something that happened today.","keyIdea":"Your point of view is how things look and feel to you. Other people have their own. To see their side, ask: how would I feel if that happened to me? Then think about what they know, what they want and what has happened to them before. The same moment can feel very different to two people, and seeing both sides helps you choose kind, fair words.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Does this thought look at the other person's side, or only at mine? Drag each one, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["👟 Sees their side","🙈 Only my side"],"items":[["How would I feel if that were me?",0],["Maybe it was an accident.",0],["You look upset. Are you OK?",0],["What do you want to do?",0],["I want it, so it is mine.",1],["My way is the only way.",1],["You are just being silly.",1],["I don't care how you feel.",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagpov","caption":"Flagship lab: give feelings, guess where someone looks, sort both sides, meet one dog and pick the kind reply.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_nJB6KA2vyo":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"The cat purrs and leans on your hand. What does it mean?","choices":["It likes it","It wants you to stop","It is cross"],"answer":0,"hint":"Purring and leaning in is a happy sign.","explain":"It likes it. You can keep stroking slowly and softly."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"The cat puts its ears flat. What do you do?","choices":["Stop","Stroke it faster","Pick it up"],"answer":0,"hint":"Flat ears are a sign. Is it a yes or a no?","explain":"Stop. Flat ears mean the cat has had enough."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"You find {n} worms on the path. What is kind?","choices":["Put them on the soil","Keep them in a pocket","Squash them"],"answer":0,"hint":"Where do worms live? Think of the garden.","explain":"Put the {n} worms back on the soil. That is where worms live.","params":{"n":[2,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"How can you help a plant?","choices":["Give it some water","Pull off its leaves","Stamp on it"],"answer":0,"hint":"Plants are alive. What do they need?","explain":"Give it some water. Plants need water and light to grow."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Your friend has the bike. You want a go. What do you say?","choices":["Can I have a turn, please?","Get off! It's mine!","Push them off"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which one uses kind words?","explain":"Can I have a turn, please? Grabbing and pushing can hurt your friend."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"You bump into {n} children by mistake. What do you say?","choices":["Sorry! Are you OK?","Ha ha!","Nothing"],"answer":0,"hint":"What do we say when we hurt someone by mistake?","explain":"Sorry! Are you OK? Even by mistake, we stop and check the {n} children are all right.","params":{"n":[2,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"A ladybird lands on your hand. What do you do?","choices":["Let it walk off onto a leaf","Squeeze it","Shake it off hard"],"answer":0,"hint":"Ladybirds are small and soft.","explain":"Let it walk off onto a leaf. Be still and gentle, so it is not hurt."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"How do we stroke a cat?","choices":["Slowly and softly","Fast and hard","On its tail"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of soft hands. What does a cat like?","explain":"Slowly and softly, along its back. Fast, hard pats can hurt and make the cat run."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"You see {n} snails on the path. What is kind?","choices":["Put them back on the leaves","Put them in a jar","Step on them"],"answer":0,"hint":"Where do snails live? Look under the leaves.","explain":"Put the {n} snails back on the leaves, where they live. A jar is not their home.","params":{"n":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"The dog walks away from you. What do you do?","choices":["Let it go","Run after it","Pull it back"],"answer":0,"hint":"What is the dog's body saying when it walks away?","explain":"Let it go. Walking away means no more. It can come back when it wants to."},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Your friend gives you {n} stickers. What do you say?","choices":["Thank you!","More!","Nothing"],"answer":0,"hint":"What do we say when someone is kind to us?","explain":"Thank you! Your friend shared {n} stickers, so we say thank you.","params":{"n":[2,5]}}],"explain":{"kid":["Gentle means soft and slow. Soft hands. Kind words.","Stroke a cat or dog slowly, along its back. If it puts its ears flat or walks away, stop. It is saying no more.","A worm, a snail and a ladybird are small and soft. Do not squash them. Put them back where they live.","Plants are alive. Give them a drink of water. Leave the flowers on. With friends, say please and thank you, and ask: are you OK?"],"adult":["At three and four, children are learning to manage their own hands and to notice how others feel. The EYFS framework covers this in Personal, Social and Emotional Development (building relationships, managing self) and in Understanding the World, where children learn to care for and respect living things, from pets and minibeasts to plants. Development Matters lists showing care for living things as part of this age range.","Gentle touch is best taught by showing: take your child's hand and stroke the pet slowly with a flat palm, from head to tail. Name what the animal's body says. A purr or leaning in means yes; flat ears, a swishing tail, a growl or walking away mean stop. Teaching children to stop when an animal moves away also protects them from bites and scratches.","Common mistakes: patting hard and fast because the child is excited; following or picking up a pet that has walked off; keeping a minibeast in a jar or pocket to play with; picking flowers or pulling leaves; and grabbing toys instead of asking. Praise the gentle choice straight away, because young children learn more from praise than from telling off.","In the lab your child strokes a cat slowly (a fast swipe makes it run), reads three cat moods, carries a worm, snail and ladybird home, chooses kind things for a plant, and picks kind words in four friend moments."]},"familyTip":"Practise 'gentle hands' on a soft toy first: slow strokes, head to tail. Then, with a calm pet, stop together when it walks away and say 'it's saying no more'.","keyIdea":"Being gentle means soft hands and kind words. Stroke a pet slowly and softly, and stop when its body says no: flat ears, a swishing tail or walking away. Minibeasts are small and soft, so carry them back to where they live. Plants are alive too: give them water and light, and leave the flowers to grow. With friends, say please, thank you, are you OK and sorry.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Is it gentle or rough? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["🤲 Gentle","✋ Not gentle"],"items":[["Stroke the cat slowly",0],["Put the worm back on the soil",0],["Water the plant",0],["Say: can I have a turn, please?",0],["Pull the cat's tail",1],["Squash a snail",1],["Pick all the flowers",1],["Grab a toy",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flaggentle","caption":"Flagship lab: stroke the cat softly, listen to its body, carry minibeasts home, help a plant grow and choose kind words.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_S7UTAhptLi":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"What is compassion?","choices":["Trying to understand someone, then doing something kind","Feeling sorry for someone from far away","Deciding someone is bad"],"answer":0,"hint":"Compassion has two parts: understanding and doing.","explain":"Compassion is understanding someone's feelings and then doing something kind. Just feeling sorry from far away is pity."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"'Her coat is old, so she must be messy.' What is this thought?","choices":["A judgement","Compassion","A fact"],"answer":0,"hint":"Does the thought know her story, or decide something bad about her?","explain":"It is a judgement. An old coat can mean her family is short of money, which is not her fault and says nothing about her."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"What is a young carer?","choices":["A child who helps look after someone at home","A child who has a pet","A child who is very kind at school"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about Kofi and his dad.","explain":"A young carer is a child who helps look after someone at home, like a parent who is ill or disabled."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Can you tell which children live in poverty by looking at them?","choices":["No, you can't tell by looking","Yes, by their clothes","Yes, by how they talk"],"answer":0,"hint":"In the lab, did the guesses match?","explain":"No. Poverty does not show on the outside, so judging by clothes or looks is unfair and often wrong."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Lily leaves school early {n} Tuesdays in a row. Which is the fair thought?","choices":["There may be a reason I can't see","She is skipping lessons","She thinks school is boring"],"answer":0,"hint":"You see what she does. Do you know why?","explain":"There may be a reason, like a hospital check-up. Leaving early {n} Tuesdays in a row does not tell you why.","params":{"n":[2,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Yusuf arrived from another country {n} days ago and is still learning English. How can you explain a game to him?","choices":["Show the rules with your hands, and use simple words","Talk louder and faster","Tell him to learn English first"],"answer":0,"hint":"What helps someone follow when the words are new?","explain":"Showing with your hands and simple words helps him follow. Louder and faster makes a new language harder.","params":{"n":[3,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Why is it unfair to judge someone for their circumstances?","choices":["They often did not choose them, and you don't know their whole story","Because circumstances are the same for everyone","Because only grown-ups have circumstances"],"answer":0,"hint":"Can a child choose to be a young carer or to be poor?","explain":"Children rarely choose their circumstances, and you only see a little of anyone's life. So compassion fits better than judgement."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Omar hardly talks in class. He moved here from another country {n} weeks ago. What is the most likely reason?","choices":["He is still learning English","He is rude","He does not like anyone"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about his circumstances. What changed for him {n} weeks ago?","explain":"He is still learning English. Talking in a new language is hard, so being quiet is not rudeness. You could help by smiling and using simple words.","params":{"n":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"Kofi looks after his dad most evenings. His homework is late {n} times this term. What is the kind way to think?","choices":["His evenings are full of caring jobs","He is lazy","He does not care about school"],"answer":0,"hint":"Kofi is a young carer. What fills his evenings?","explain":"His evenings are full of caring jobs, so late homework {n} times is not laziness. Calling him lazy is a judgement.","params":{"n":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"A class in the UK has 30 children. About how many of them live in poverty?","choices":["About 9","About 1","About 25"],"answer":0,"hint":"It is about 3 in every 10 children.","explain":"About 9, because roughly 3 in every 10 UK children live in poverty. You can't tell which ones by looking."},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Nadia uses a wheelchair. Your group is playing tag on the bumpy grass. What is the best change?","choices":["Play on the flat playground so she can join in","Play without her because it is easier","Tell her to watch"],"answer":0,"hint":"Change the game, not who is allowed to play.","explain":"Moving to the flat playground lets Nadia join in. Leaving her out because she is different is not fair."}],"explain":{"kid":["Every child has a different life. Some live with one grown-up, and some live with lots. Some use a wheelchair. Some moved here from far away and are still learning English. These things are called circumstances. Most of them were not chosen.","You can see what a child does, but not always why. Jade yawns in class. She might be lazy, or she might help look after her mum at night. A child who helps care for someone at home is called a young carer.","Some families do not have enough money for the things most people have. This is called poverty. In the UK it is about 9 children in a class of 30. You can't tell who by looking, and it is never the child's fault.","Judgement is deciding someone is bad before you know their story. Compassion is trying to understand, then doing something kind. You can ask, 'Do you want to play?', or change a game so everyone can join in."],"adult":["This topic sits in PSHE Relationships education at Key Stage 2 and the IB PYP PSPE identity strand. Children learn that families and lives differ, including disability, poverty, young caring, illness and being new to the country, and that differences in circumstances call for respect and inclusion rather than judgement.","A common mistake is reading a visible behaviour as a character trait: tired means lazy, quiet means rude, old clothes mean careless. Another is believing you can tell who is poor by looking. A third is pity in place of compassion: feeling sorry from a distance, or helping without asking, which can take away a child's choice and dignity.","The facts are real. About 30% of UK children (4.3 million in 2022–23) live in relative poverty after housing costs, roughly 9 in a class of 30. Many young carers are of primary age. Families rarely tell school about either, so the safe stance is to assume there may be a reason you can't see.","In the lab your child matches behaviours to hidden reasons, swipes thoughts to judgement or compassion, compares a day with Kofi's, a young carer, tries and fails to spot poverty by looking, and changes a tag game so Nadia, who uses a wheelchair, and Yusuf, who is new to English, can join in."]},"familyTip":"When your child says someone is 'weird' or 'lazy', ask: 'What might be going on for them that we can't see?' Then plan one kind thing together, like inviting a new child to play.","keyIdea":"Children's lives can be very different. Some have a disability, some are young carers, some live in poverty, and some are new to the country and still learning the language. These are their circumstances: things they did not choose and you often can't see. What you see a child do may have a reason behind it. Judgement decides something bad about a person without knowing their story; compassion tries to understand and then does something kind, like changing a game so everyone can join in.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Judgement or compassion? Drag each thought, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["🧱 Judgement","💛 Compassion"],"items":[["She is always late. She doesn't care.",0],["His lunch is small. He must be greedy at home.",0],["He talks funny, so he isn't clever.",0],["She can't walk, so she can't be on the team.",0],["Maybe something is hard at home. I'll be friendly.",1],["I could show him the way to the hall.",1],["Let's pick a game she can play too.",1],["I'll ask if he wants to play.",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flaglives","caption":"Flagship lab: find the reason behind what you see, swipe judgement from compassion, live a young carer's day, and change a game so all can join.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_wRlf0g2MbB":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"What does empathy mean?","choices":["Feeling with someone: imagining how they feel","Feeling sorry for someone","Being the same as someone"],"answer":0,"hint":"Empathy feels with. Sympathy feels for.","explain":"Empathy is imagining how someone feels and staying with them. Feeling sorry for them is sympathy."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Nia says, 'Oh dear, poor you,' and walks on. What is that?","choices":["Sympathy","Empathy","Care"],"answer":0,"hint":"Did Nia stay and imagine the feeling?","explain":"Sympathy: Nia feels sorry, but does not stay or imagine the feeling. Empathy would sound like 'I'd be upset too.'"},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Tom has {n} brothers and Ali has none. Is that a way they are similar or different?","choices":["Different","Similar","Kind"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is it something they share?","explain":"Different. Tom has {n} brothers and Ali has none, so it is not the same. They can still be similar in other ways.","params":{"n":[1,4]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"What is a community?","choices":["A group of people who share a place and help each other","One best friend","A game with rules"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of your class or your street.","explain":"A community is a group of people who share a place, like a class, a street or a club."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"A new girl stands alone at playtime. What would help her belong?","choices":["Ask her name and invite her to play","Wait for her to ask","Tell her to go away"],"answer":0,"hint":"Inclusion means inviting people in.","explain":"Asking her name and inviting her to play helps her belong. Waiting leaves her outside the group."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"There are {n} stickers for {n} children. What is fair?","choices":["One sticker each","All of them for one child","Two for the first child"],"answer":0,"hint":"Fair means an equal chance for everyone.","explain":"One each is fair: {n} stickers shared by {n} children gives everyone the same.","params":{"n":[3,8]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Kai helps his grandad carry the shopping. Which word fits?","choices":["Care","Perspective","Different"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which word means looking after someone?","explain":"Care. Kai is looking after his grandad by helping him."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Zac falls over. Amy says, 'Ouch, I'd cry too. Let me help you up.' What is Amy showing?","choices":["Empathy","Sympathy","Perspective"],"answer":0,"hint":"Does Amy imagine Zac's feeling and stay with him?","explain":"Empathy. Amy imagines how Zac feels ('I'd cry too') and stays to help. Just saying 'poor you' would be sympathy."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"Ben and Lucy both have {n} letters in their name. Is that a way they are similar or different?","choices":["Similar","Different","Fair"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is it something they share?","explain":"Similar. Both names have {n} letters, so they share it. Similar means alike in some way.","params":{"n":[3,7]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"{n} children want the red bike. What is the fair way?","choices":["Everyone takes a turn","The biggest child has it all day","The first child keeps it"],"answer":0,"hint":"Fair means everyone gets an equal chance.","explain":"Taking turns is fair, because all {n} children get a go. Keeping it all day gives one child more than the others.","params":{"n":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"You see a cat on your side of a box. Your friend sits on the other side. What can you say?","choices":["She might see something different","She must see a cat too","She sees nothing"],"answer":0,"hint":"What you see depends on where you sit.","explain":"She might see something different. That is her perspective: how things look from where she is."}],"explain":{"kid":["Mia and Raj are both 7. They both have a dog. That makes them similar. Mia loves pizza and Raj plays cricket. That makes them different. Two people can be similar and different at the same time.","Leo drops his ice cream and cries. Empathy is when you think, 'I would be sad too,' and you stay with him. Sympathy is when you just feel sorry for him. Empathy feels with. Sympathy feels for.","Perspective is how things look from where you are. If you sit on one side of a toy, you see one side. Your friend on the other side sees something else. Feelings work like that too.","Fair means everyone gets a go. Kind means gentle words and help. Care means looking after someone. A community is a group who share a place, like your class. Everyone should feel they belong."],"adult":["This vocabulary sits in PSHE Relationships education at Key Stage 1 and in the IB PYP PSPE interactions strand. Children learn to use eight words: empathy, perspective, kind, fair, community, similar, different and care. They also start to tell empathy from sympathy, and use belonging and inclusion about their class.","A common mistake is to treat empathy and sympathy as the same. Sympathy is feeling sorry for someone, often from a distance. Empathy is imagining their feeling and staying with them. Another slip is thinking 'different' means 'not alike at all'. Two people can share some things and not others.","Young children often think others see what they see. Piaget's three-mountains task showed that taking another person's view grows with age. So start with something concrete, like a block with a different picture on each side, before talking about feelings. At this age, 'fair' means equal turns and equal chances.","In the lab your child sorts facts about Mia and Raj into a Venn diagram, sorts replies to Leo into empathy and sympathy, sits in three seats round a picture block, gives fair turns on a swing, and drags kind actions so a new girl, Sofi, joins the class community."]},"familyTip":"When a character in a book is upset, ask: 'How would you feel? What could you say to show empathy?' Then find one way you are similar to a friend and one way you are different.","keyIdea":"Some words help us understand other people. Similar means alike in some way; different means not the same, and two people can be both. Empathy is feeling with someone: imagining their feeling and staying with them. Sympathy is feeling sorry for them. Perspective is how things look from where someone is. Fair means everyone gets an equal chance. Kind and care mean being gentle and helping. A community is a group of people who share a place, and inclusion makes sure everyone belongs.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Empathy or sympathy? Drag each reply, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["🫶 Empathy: feel with","😔 Sympathy: feel sorry for"],"items":[["I'd be scared too. I'll come with you.",0],["You look sad. Do you want a hug?",0],["That must hurt. I'll sit with you.",0],["I know how that feels. Shall we fix it?",0],["Oh, poor you.",1],["What a pity.",1],["That's sad for you.",1],["Bad luck.",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagothers","caption":"Flagship lab: sort Mia and Raj in a Venn diagram, tell empathy from sympathy, sit in three seats, give fair turns, and help Sofi belong.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_2oswCNuapH":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"What is a stereotype?","choices":["A fixed idea that everyone in a group is the same","A fact about one person","A rule that keeps people safe"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of boys don't cry. Who is it about?","explain":"A stereotype is a fixed idea that everyone in a group is the same. It ignores individual differences."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Why are stereotypes unfair?","choices":["They ignore individual differences","They are always about bad things","They are only said by adults"],"answer":0,"hint":"Remember the twelve boys in the lab.","explain":"People in any group are all different. A stereotype treats them as copies, so it is wrong about many of them."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"People who wear glasses are clever. Is this a stereotype?","choices":["Yes, it labels a whole group","No, it is a compliment","No, because it is nice"],"answer":0,"hint":"Does it say everyone in a group is the same?","explain":"Yes. Even a compliment is a stereotype if it puts one label on a whole group."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Ella says girls can't catch. What is the best reply?","choices":["Priya catches loads in netball. Let her try.","You're so mean!","Say nothing."],"answer":0,"hint":"Use a counter-example and stay calm.","explain":"Naming Priya is a calm counter-example. Insults start a fight and silence lets the stereotype stand."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Someone says boys don't cry. Which fact breaks it?","choices":["Leo, 10, cried when his dog died","Sam, 9, plays football","Some girls cry"],"answer":0,"hint":"You need a boy who cries.","explain":"Leo is a boy who cried, so he is a counter-example. Girls crying says nothing about boys."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"In a class of 30, {n} children love chess. Is it a stereotype to say some children in my class like chess?","choices":["No, it is a true fact about some people","Yes, it is about a group","Yes, because chess is a game"],"answer":0,"hint":"Does it say every child is the same?","explain":"No. Some is not all, and {n} children really do like chess, so it does not label the whole class.","params":{"n":[3,9]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"Twelve boys are labelled loves football, but only {k} of them do. For how many boys is the label wrong?","answer":"12-k","hint":"Take the boys it fits away from all twelve.","explain":"12 − {k} = {=12-k}. The label is wrong for {=12-k} boys, which is why stereotypes are unfair.","params":{"k":[3,8]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Girls' and boys' maths scores in one class overlap from 4 to 19. What does that show?","choices":["You can't tell a person's score from their group","Boys always score higher","Girls always score higher"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of the mixed-up line in the lab.","explain":"The scores mix all the way through, so knowing someone is a girl or a boy tells you almost nothing about their score."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Which of these is a stereotype?","choices":["Girls aren't good at maths.","Amira is good at maths.","Our class did a maths test today."],"answer":0,"hint":"A stereotype puts one idea on a whole group of people.","explain":"Girls aren't good at maths says a whole group is the same. The others are about one person or a fact."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"A class has {n} boys. Someone says all boys love football. How many boys must dislike football to break that idea?","choices":["Just 1","All {n}","Half of them"],"answer":0,"hint":"How many counter-examples does it take to show an all idea is wrong?","explain":"Just 1. One boy who doesn't love football is a counter-example, so all boys love football is false, even in a class of {n}.","params":{"n":[10,16]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Someone says old people can't use phones. Which is a counter-example?","choices":["Grandad Ray, 74, runs a video channel","Nan Rose, 80, loves knitting","Phones are hard to use"],"answer":0,"hint":"Find a real old person who uses phones or tech.","explain":"Grandad Ray is old and uses tech every day, so he proves the idea wrong. Knitting says nothing about phones."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"In one class, girls' maths scores run from {a} to 19 out of 20. What is the gap between the lowest and highest girl?","answer":"19-a","hint":"Take the lowest score away from the highest.","explain":"19 − {a} = {=19-a}. A gap of {=19-a} inside one group is much bigger than the 0.2 gap between the girls' and boys' averages.","params":{"a":[3,8]}}],"explain":{"kid":["A stereotype is an idea that everyone in a group is the same. Boys don't cry. Girls aren't good at maths. Old people can't use phones. The group might be about gender, age, race or religion.","Stereotypes are unfair because people are not copies. Some boys love football, and some love ballet or chess. The label fits a few people and is wrong about the rest. That is called ignoring individual differences.","You can break a stereotype with a counter-example. That is one real person who does not fit. Amira is a girl who won the maths contest. Leo is a boy who cried when his dog died. One person is enough to show an all idea is wrong.","Watch out for nice ones too. People who wear glasses are clever sounds kind, but it is still one label for a whole group. When you hear a stereotype, stay calm and name a person it leaves out."],"adult":["This topic is part of the IB PYP PSPE identity strand. It also fits PSHE Relationships lessons at Key Stage 2. Pupils learn that stereotypes can be based on gender, race, religion, age or disability. They learn that stereotypes are unfair and can hurt. The skills are to define a stereotype, say why one is wrong, and challenge it with a counter-example.","The key idea is variation. In the lab's sample class, girls' maths scores run from 4 to 19 and boys' from 3 to 19. Yet the two averages differ by only 0.2. Real data looks like this too. A 2008 study in Science looked at over seven million US pupils aged about 7 to 17. It found no real gap between girls and boys in maths tests.","Common mistakes: thinking a stereotype is fine if it is true of some people, and thinking a compliment can't be a stereotype. Another is a weak counter-example. Some boys like maths does not answer girls can't do maths. A fourth is answering with insults, which makes views harder. Studies of stereotype threat show labels can pull down the scores of the people they describe.","In the lab your child flips twelve boys labelled loves football and counts how many fit. They drag the right person onto three stereotypes and slide two groups of maths scores into one line. Then they find four stereotypes in a list of eight and pick calm replies to one said out loud."]},"familyTip":"When an advert, a film or a friend says 'boys always…' or 'girls never…', ask your child: 'Can you think of one person who doesn't fit?' Naming a real person is the quickest way to break a stereotype.","keyIdea":"A stereotype is a fixed, oversimplified idea that everyone in a group is the same, for example because of their gender, race, age or religion. Stereotypes are unfair because they ignore individual differences: people inside any group differ far more from each other than the group differs from another group. One counter-example, a real person who does not fit, shows an all-or-never idea is wrong. Nice-sounding stereotypes still put a label on people.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Stereotype or not? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["🏷️ Stereotype: one label for a whole group","👤 Not a stereotype: a person or a fact"],"items":[["Boys don't cry.",0],["Girls are bad at football.",0],["Old people can't learn new things.",0],["Everyone with glasses is clever.",0],["Leo cried at the end of the film.",1],["Our school has 210 pupils.",1],["Some children in my class like chess.",1],["Amira is in the maths club.",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagstereo","caption":"Flagship lab: flip labelled cards, break stereotypes with counter-examples, mix two groups' scores and reply calmly.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_HFN1pGASpZ":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"A club will not let a girl join because she is deaf. What is this?","choices":["Discrimination","Only prejudice","Fair, because it is their club"],"answer":0,"hint":"Did someone act, and was it because of who she is?","explain":"The club acted and treated her worse because of her disability. That is discrimination, and the Equality Act protects disability."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Priya gets extra time in tests because she has dyslexia. Is that unfair to the others?","choices":["No, it gives her a fair chance","Yes, it is discrimination","Yes, everyone must be treated the same"],"answer":0,"hint":"What is the extra time for?","explain":"The extra time helps her show what she knows. Support for a need is fair; it is not discrimination against anyone."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Kofi is followed round a shop {n} times this month, though he has never taken anything. Why is this discrimination?","choices":["He is treated worse because of his race","He goes to the shop a lot","Guards follow everyone"],"answer":0,"hint":"Has he done anything to be followed for?","explain":"He has done nothing wrong in {n} visits. He is watched because he is Black, so he is treated worse for who he is.","params":{"n":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Some children mock a boy’s religion. The next day he does not want to come to school. What does this show?","choices":["Discrimination causes real harm","It was only a joke","He is too sensitive"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at what happened to him afterwards.","explain":"He is so hurt that he wants to stay away. Remarks about who someone is cause real harm, even when called a joke."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Your friend is left out because of her skin colour. What is a safe, helpful first step?","choices":["Include her and tell a trusted adult","Shout at the others","Stay out of it"],"answer":0,"hint":"Choose something that helps her and keeps you safe.","explain":"Including her shows she is not alone, and an adult can stop it. Shouting can start a fight, and staying out leaves her alone."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"{n} children see a boy being mocked for his race. {k} of them step in safely to help. How many are still only watching?","answer":"n-k","hint":"Take the helpers away from everyone who saw it.","explain":"{n} − {k} = {=n-k} are still only watching. Each of them could also help safely, for example by telling an adult.","params":{"n":[4,9],"k":[1,3]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Which one is a protected characteristic in the Equality Act 2010?","choices":["Religion or belief","Favourite football team","Hair colour"],"answer":0,"hint":"The Act lists 9, including age, race and disability.","explain":"Religion or belief is one of the 9. Being unkind about a team or hair colour is still wrong, but those are not on the list."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Which shows the chain in the right order?","choices":["Stereotype, then prejudice, then discrimination","Discrimination, then stereotype, then prejudice","Prejudice, then discrimination, then stereotype"],"answer":0,"hint":"Start with an idea, then a feeling, then an action.","explain":"A stereotype is an idea, prejudice is the feeling it can feed, and discrimination is the unfair action that can follow."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Ella does not like Muslim children, but she has never said or done anything about it. What is this?","choices":["Prejudice","Discrimination","A fair opinion"],"answer":0,"hint":"Has anyone been treated worse yet, or is it still a feeling?","explain":"It is prejudice: a feeling against people because of their religion. It becomes discrimination if she treats someone worse."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"A poster shows {n} of the protected characteristics in the Equality Act 2010. How many are missing from the poster?","answer":"9-n","hint":"The Act lists 9 protected characteristics in total.","explain":"There are 9 in the Act, so 9 − {n} = {=9-n} are missing.","params":{"n":[3,8]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Jas is not picked for the football team because he did not come to the try-out. Is this discrimination?","choices":["No, it is about what he did","Yes, he was treated differently","Yes, being left out is always discrimination"],"answer":0,"hint":"Ask: is it because of who he is?","explain":"Missing the try-out is about what he did, not who he is. That is a fair reason, so it is not discrimination."},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"After hearing {n} jokes about one group, Tom starts to dislike children from that group without knowing any of them. What is this feeling?","choices":["Prejudice","Discrimination","A fact"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is it something he feels, or something he does to someone?","explain":"It is prejudice. The {n} jokes fed a stereotype, and the stereotype fed a feeling against people he has never met.","params":{"n":[2,6]}}],"explain":{"kid":["The word prejudice means to pre-judge: to decide what someone is like before you know them. It is a feeling, like not liking the new boy because of where his family comes from.","Discrimination is what someone does. It is treating a person worse because of who they are. A club that will not let a deaf girl join is discriminating. So is a guard who follows a boy round a shop just because he is Black.","Not every difference is unfair. If Sam misses the try-out, it is fair not to pick him. If Priya gets extra time because she has dyslexia, that helps her have a fair chance. Ask: is it because of who they are?","Discrimination does real harm. The person can feel hurt, alone and scared to come to school. Their friends and family hurt too, and the whole class trusts each other less. You can help: include them, say calmly that it is not fair, and tell an adult."],"adult":["This topic sits in PSHE and the statutory Relationships Education guidance, which asks pupils to understand respect for others who are different and how stereotypes can be unfair. Pupils of 9 to 11 should define prejudice and discrimination in their own words. They should describe the harm it does and name an action they can take.","The terms build in a chain. A stereotype is a fixed idea about a group. Prejudice is a negative attitude or feeling toward a person because of their group. Discrimination is unfair treatment because of who someone is. It is an action, so it can happen without anyone saying a word. The Equality Act 2010 names nine protected characteristics: age, disability, gender reassignment, marriage and civil partnership, pregnancy and maternity, race, religion or belief, sex, and sexual orientation.","Common mistakes: calling a private feeling discrimination, when it is prejudice until someone acts on it; thinking any different treatment is discrimination, when the question is why; treating support such as extra time as unfair; and calling hurtful remarks just a joke. A common error with older children is to think that standing up means being a hero. Be careful to stress safe actions: include, speak up calmly, report.","In the lab, learners drag cards to build the chain from idea to feeling to action. They sort cases by whether the reason is who the person is. They open the ripples of harm round one girl and shield the nine protected characteristics. Last, they choose the safe, helpful responses to two scenes, one of them online."]},"familyTip":"When a news story, film or playground tale involves someone being treated unfairly, ask: was it because of who they are, or something they did? Then ask: what could someone nearby have done to help, safely?","keyIdea":"Prejudice is judging or disliking someone before you know them, because of a group they belong to, such as their race, religion, disability or sex. It is a feeling or attitude. Discrimination is an action: treating someone worse because of who they are. A stereotype (an idea about a whole group) can feed prejudice, and prejudice can lead to discrimination. Being treated differently is not always discrimination; the test is whether it is because of who they are. Discrimination hurts the person, the people around them and the whole community. In Great Britain the Equality Act 2010 protects nine characteristics by law. You can stand against it safely: include the person, calmly say it is not fair, and tell a trusted adult.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Prejudice or discrimination? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["💭 Prejudice: a feeling or attitude","✋ Discrimination: an unfair action"],"items":[["Dislikes the new girl because she is from Romania",0],["Won’t pass the ball to the boy in a turban",1],["Feels nervous of old people without knowing any",0],["Tells a girl she can’t join because she is deaf",1],["Thinks badly of a family because of their religion",0],["Pays a woman less than a man for the same job",1],["Distrusts someone because of their skin colour",0],["Refuses to let a wheelchair user into the club",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagprej","caption":"Flagship lab: build idea → feeling → action, test the reason, open the ripples of harm, shield the 9 protected characteristics and choose safe ways to help.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_cOknxrYhwL":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"What is confirmation bias?","choices":["Noticing what fits your idea and missing what does not","Checking every fact very carefully","Agreeing with a friend to be kind"],"answer":0,"hint":"It is about which evidence your mind keeps and which it lets slip.","explain":"Confirmation bias means seeking and remembering what fits a belief while skipping the rest."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m2","prompt":"Max was early on {n} days and late on 2 days. Someone who thinks Max is lazy remembers only the late days. How many days do they forget?","answer":"n","hint":"Which days do not fit the idea that Max is lazy?","explain":"They forget the {n} early days, because those do not fit the idea. That is confirmation bias.","params":{"n":[5,12]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Which question best checks your own bias?","choices":["Would I think the same about someone else?","How can I prove I am right?","Who agrees with me?"],"answer":0,"hint":"One question swaps the person to test whether the judgement is fair.","explain":"The flip test swaps the person. If your view would change, it rests on who they are, not what they did."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Who has unconscious biases?","choices":["Everyone does","Only unkind people","Only adults"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about how every brain makes quick guesses to save time.","explain":"Everyone does, because every brain makes quick guesses. Being aware of them is what lets you check them."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"Leah judged the new boy after {n} minutes on his first day. The school day is 360 minutes. How many minutes of that day had she not seen yet?","answer":"360-n","hint":"Take the minutes she saw away from the whole day.","explain":"360 − {n} = {=360-n} minutes she had not seen. A first impression uses very little evidence.","params":{"n":[2,30]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Which thought is based on evidence?","choices":["Ravi has scored in every match, so he is good at football","Ella wears glasses, so she must be clever","The new girl lives on a farm, so she will not like computers"],"answer":0,"hint":"Evidence is about what the person has actually done.","explain":"Ravi's goals are things he did. Glasses and living on a farm say nothing about being clever or liking computers."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"In Tajfel's experiments, boys were put into groups for a trivial reason. What did they tend to do?","choices":["Give their own group more points","Share points the same with everyone","Give the other group more points"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about the pull towards people on your own side.","explain":"They tended to favour their own group, even though the groups meant nothing. This is in-group bias."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"You realise you judged someone unfairly. What is the best next step?","choices":["Find out more and change your view","Hide it so no one knows","Decide you are a bad person"],"answer":0,"hint":"Questioning a bias is a habit. What do you do once you have caught one?","explain":"Finding out more and changing your view fixes the unfair judgement. Hiding or shame leaves it in place."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"What is an assumption?","choices":["Something you believe without checking","A fact you have proved","A rule the school has made"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about whether you have looked for evidence or not.","explain":"An assumption is believed without checking. It might be right or wrong, which is why it is worth questioning."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"You think Mia is bossy. You notice {a} bossy things she did, but miss {b} kind things. How many things did Mia really do?","answer":"a+b","hint":"Add the things you noticed and the things you missed.","explain":"{a} + {b} = {=a+b} things. Seeing only {a} of them is confirmation bias at work.","params":{"a":[2,6],"b":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Kai is quiet on his first day. Which thought shows you are questioning an assumption?","choices":["Maybe he is shy or new. I will say hi and find out.","He is quiet, so he must be unfriendly.","Quiet people never make good friends."],"answer":0,"hint":"Which thought looks for more evidence before judging?","explain":"Saying hi to find out tests the first impression. The other two jump from one quiet day to a fixed judgement."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"In a sharing game you give {a} of 10 tokens to a stranger with your own sticker colour. How many more tokens does your colour get than the other colour?","answer":"2*a-10","hint":"First find how many tokens the other colour gets, then find the difference.","explain":"The other colour gets 10 − {a} = {=10-a}, so your colour gets {a} − {=10-a} = {=2*a-10} more. Favouring your own random group is in-group bias.","params":{"a":[6,9]}}],"explain":{"kid":["An assumption is something you believe without checking. We all make them. Your brain makes quick guesses to save time, often before you notice. When a quick guess leans for or against a person, that is a bias. A bias you are not aware of is called an unconscious bias.","First impressions can trick you. Kai is quiet on his first day, so you might think he is unfriendly. But he could be shy, or still learning English. One quiet day is not enough evidence. When Kai lends you a pencil and asks to play, your view should change.","Biases can grow. Once you believe an idea, you notice the things that fit it and skip the things that do not. That is confirmation bias. We also tend to favour people who seem like us, even when the group is only a sticker colour. That is in-group bias.","You cannot switch bias off, but you can catch it. Notice the snap thought and pause. Ask: what is my evidence? Would I think the same about someone else? This is called the flip test. Then check by finding out more, or by asking the person. Do it every time, not once."],"adult":["This topic fits the PSHE Association plan for Key Stage 2, under respectful relationships and living in the wider world. Pupils learn to spot and challenge stereotypes and assumptions. They learn to respect people who are not like them. The DfE guidance on Relationships Education asks for the same. The focus here is on checking your own thinking, not judging other people.","Useful terms: assumption, first impression, snap judgement, evidence, bias, unconscious or implicit bias, confirmation bias, in-group bias, stereotype and perspective. Confirmation bias is the habit of seeking and keeping what fits a belief; Nickerson reviewed it in 1998. In 1970 Tajfel put boys into groups for a trivial reason. They still gave their own group more points. Keep claims modest: tests that say they measure a person's hidden bias are still debated.","Common mistakes: thinking only unkind people have biases; thinking you can be free of bias just by deciding to; treating a nice assumption, such as quiet children are clever, as harmless; mixing up what someone did with a guess about who they are; and feeling so ashamed of a bias that you hide it. Be careful to show checking as a habit for life. It leads to fairer choices and more empathy.","In the lab the learner drags a guess about how friendly quiet new Kai is. Then they open four clues and move it. They tap eight things Mia did while a bossy filter fades the kind ones, and count what fits. They share ten tokens between two strangers sorted by a random sticker colour. They flip four snap thoughts to judge evidence or assumption, and put notice, pause, ask and check in order."]},"familyTip":"Over dinner, each person shares one first impression they had about someone that turned out wrong, and what changed their mind. Then try the flip test on a headline or a TV character together.","keyIdea":"An assumption is something you believe without checking. Everyone's brain makes quick guesses about people, often without noticing; when those guesses lean for or against someone, that is bias, and a bias you are not aware of is unconscious bias. First impressions rest on very little evidence. Confirmation bias makes you notice what fits an idea and miss what does not, and in-group bias pulls you towards people like you, even in a random group. You cannot switch bias off, but you can catch it: notice the snap thought, pause, ask what your evidence is and whether you would think the same about someone else, then check by finding out more.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Evidence or assumption? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["📊 Based on evidence","🙈 An assumption"],"items":[["Zara has helped tidy up after every art lesson, so she is helpful.",0],["Tom has been on time every day this month, so he is punctual.",0],["Priya won the last three chess games, so she is a strong player.",0],["He is tall, so he must be good at basketball.",1],["She is quiet, so she must be stuck-up.",1],["They live in a big house, so they must be spoilt.",1],["He wears glasses, so he must be clever.",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagbias","caption":"Flagship lab: revise a first impression, see what a bossy filter hides, share tokens across random teams, flip snap thoughts, then notice, pause, ask, check.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_OJVkWvIaM_":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Ben uses a wheelchair. Tom does not. What could be the same about them?","choices":["They both like football","Nothing at all","They are the same in every way"],"answer":0,"hint":"People who look different can still like the same things.","explain":"Ben and Tom can both like football. Being different in one way does not stop you sharing other things."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Zofia says \"Cześć!\" It means hello in Polish. What is a kind thing to say?","choices":["\"Cześć! Can you teach me more?\"","\"Talk normal.\"","\"That is a funny word.\""],"answer":0,"hint":"Which one is kind and wants to find out more?","explain":"Asking to learn more is kind and curious. Polish is normal for Zofia."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Mia and Sam both like to paint. Where does \"Likes to paint\" go in their Venn diagram?","choices":["In the middle","Only in Mia's circle","Outside both circles"],"answer":0,"hint":"Where do the two circles cross?","explain":"Things both of them like go in the middle, where the circles cross."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m4","prompt":"Leo lives with his dad and {s} sisters. How many people live in Leo's home, with Leo too?","answer":"s+2","hint":"Count Leo, his dad and each sister.","explain":"Leo, his dad and {s} sisters make {=s+2} people. That is Leo's family.","params":{"s":[1,4]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Jun eats with chopsticks. What is true?","choices":["It is a different way to eat","It is the wrong way to eat","Only grown-ups can do it"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is there only one right way to eat?","explain":"Chopsticks are just a different way to eat. Lots of people use them every day."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"What do Diwali and Christmas both have?","choices":["Special food with family","A tree with lights","Red lanterns"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which one is on both days?","explain":"Both have special food with family and friends. A tree is for Christmas, and clay lamps are for Diwali."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"Mia and Sam list {t} things about themselves. {s} of them are the same. How many are different?","answer":"t-s","hint":"Take the same ones away from the whole list.","explain":"{t} take away {s} is {=t-s} things that are different.","params":{"t":[8,12],"s":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Amira wears a hijab. You want to play tag. What do you say?","choices":["\"Do you want to play tag?\"","\"Take it off to play.\"","\"You look funny.\""],"answer":0,"hint":"Can Amira play in her hijab?","explain":"Amira can play just as she is. Ask her to play."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Mia has curly hair. Sam has straight hair. Is that the same or different?","choices":["Different","The same","Both curly"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is curly hair the same as straight hair?","explain":"Curly and straight are not the same, so that is a difference."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"Mia and Sam write down {n} things they both like. How many things go in the middle of the Venn diagram?","answer":"n","hint":"The middle is for things they both like.","explain":"Things they both like go in the middle, so {n} things go there.","params":{"n":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Kit lives with her two mums. Is that a family?","choices":["Yes, it is a family","No, it needs a dad","No, it is too small"],"answer":0,"hint":"Do the people in Kit's home love and care for her?","explain":"Families are made in lots of ways. Kit and her two mums are a family."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"In a class, {a} children speak English at home and {b} speak another language at home. How many children are in the class?","answer":"a+b","hint":"Add the two groups together.","explain":"{a} add {b} is {=a+b}. Lots of children speak more than one language.","params":{"a":[10,20],"b":[2,8]}}],"explain":{"kid":["Mia and Sam are best friends. They are both 6. They both like to paint. That is the same.","Mia has curly hair. Sam has straight hair. At home Mia speaks Polish, and Sam speaks English. That is different.","Homes are not all the same. Kit lives with her two mums. Ravi lives with his gran. Leo lives with his dad and his big sister. They are all families.","Jun eats with chopsticks. That is not weird. It is a new thing to learn. Say, \"Can you show me how?\""],"adult":["This sits in personal, social and health education and in the DfE Relationships Education guidance for primary schools. Pupils learn that other families may look different from their own, and that they should respect those differences. They also learn to respect others who are very different from them, for example in looks, character or background. In the early years it links to the Early Learning Goal for People, Culture and Communities.","The key words are similarity, difference, respect, family, culture, language and celebration. A Venn diagram is a good tool at this age. It has two circles that cross, with the shared things in the middle. It shows a child that two people can share some things and differ in others at the same time.","Common mistakes: thinking a best friend must be the same in every way, or that someone who looks different shares nothing with you. Another slip is to call a different way of eating, dressing or speaking weird or wrong, or to think a real family needs a mum and a dad. Be careful not to ignore differences either; name them kindly and with interest.","In the lab your child sorts six facts about two friends into same or different, drags facts into a Venn diagram, builds three different families in a house, chooses a kind and curious reply to three differences, and finds what Diwali, Eid, Christmas and Lunar New Year all share."]},"familyTip":"Draw two big crossing circles, one for you and one for your child. Put things only you like on your side, things only they like on theirs, and things you both like in the middle. Talk about how you are the same and different.","keyIdea":"People can be the same in some ways and different in others, all at once. Best friends can both like to paint and still have different hair, pets or languages. Families are made in lots of ways, and they are all families. When someone does a thing in a different way, it is not weird or wrong: ask about it and learn something new.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Kind or unkind about a difference? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["😊 Kind and curious","😟 Unkind"],"items":[["\"Can you show me how?\"",0],["\"That is weird.\"",1],["\"How do you say hello?\"",0],["\"Talk normal.\"",1],["\"Do you want to play?\"",0],["\"You look funny.\"",1],["\"Tell me about your family.\"",0],["\"That is not a real family.\"",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagalike","caption":"Flagship lab: sort same and different, drag facts into a Venn diagram, build three families, choose kind replies and find what four special days share.","opts":{"step":0}}}});
